<span class="kicker">2026-08-08 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How Much Does a Paver Walkway Cost Installed?</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A professionally installed paver walkway typically costs between 18 and 35 dollars per square foot, with most standard residential projects landing somewhere in the middle of that range once labor, materials, base preparation, and a workmanship warranty are all factored in. </p>
<p>That per square foot number gets thrown around a lot, but it doesn’t tell you much on its own unless you understand what’s actually included in it, and what separates a complete, accurate quote from one that’s missing pieces you’ll end up paying for later.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we quote installed paver walkway projects across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, on a regular basis, and this guide breaks down exactly what goes into that installed price, what a sample quote actually looks like line by line, and how to compare quotes from different contractors without getting misled by a number that looks good but isn’t telling the whole story.</p>
<h2><b>Installed Paver Walkway Cost at a Glance</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Walkway Size</b></td><td><b>Installed Cost Range</b></td><td><b>Typical Installed Cost</b></td></tr><tr><td>50 square feet</td><td>900 to 1,750 dollars</td><td>Around 1,250 to 1,400 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>75 square feet</td><td>1,350 to 2,625 dollars</td><td>Around 1,875 to 2,100 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>100 square feet</td><td>1,800 to 3,500 dollars</td><td>Around 2,500 to 2,800 dollars</td></tr><tr><td>150 square feet</td><td>2,700 to 5,250 dollars</td><td>Around 3,750 to 4,200 dollars</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These figures reflect a standard walkway with straightforward site conditions. Curved layouts, sloped sites, intricate patterns, and challenging soil conditions all push toward the higher end of the range, sometimes above it.</p>
<h2><b>What “Installed” Actually Includes</b></h2>
<h3><b>Labor</b></h3>
<p>Labor covers the physical work of excavation, base installation and compaction, paver laying, cutting, edge restraint installation, and jointing. This is typically the largest single component of an installed price, generally accounting for close to half the total cost on a standard project.</p>
<h3><b>Materials</b></h3>
<p>Materials include the pavers themselves, gravel base material, bedding sand, polymeric sand, and edge restraints. Material costs scale directly with paver choice, standard concrete pavers cost less than brick or natural stone, and with total square footage.</p>
<h3><b>Base Preparation and Equipment</b></h3>
<p>Proper base preparation, including plate compactor use and the labor involved in compacting in layers rather than all at once, is typically bundled into the overall labor cost rather than broken out separately, but it’s worth confirming this is happening rather than assuming it. Our<a href="/blog/how-thick-should-paver-base-be-for-a-walkway/"> guide to how thick paver base should be</a> covers what proper base work actually involves and why it’s not a place to accept shortcuts.</p>
<h3><b>Site Prep and Cleanup</b></h3>
<p>This includes protecting existing landscaping during construction, hauling away excavated soil, and general site cleanup once the project is finished. Some contractors itemize this separately, while others fold it into overall labor.</p>
<h3><b>Workmanship Warranty</b></h3>
<p>A reputable installer typically includes a workmanship warranty covering settling, shifting, or other installation-related issues within a defined period after completion. This isn’t usually a separate line item cost, but it’s a meaningful part of what you’re paying for compared to a DIY installation, which comes with no such guarantee.</p>
<h2><b>Sample Itemized Quote: 100 Square Foot Walkway</b></h2>
<p>To put these categories in context, here’s roughly how a 100 square foot standard concrete paver walkway might break down in a typical itemized quote.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Line Item</b></td><td><b>Approximate Cost</b></td><td><b>Percentage of Total</b></td></tr><tr><td>Labor (excavation, base, laying, jointing)</td><td>1,200 to 1,350 dollars</td><td>45 to 50 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Pavers and materials</td><td>700 to 850 dollars</td><td>28 to 32 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Base material (gravel, sand)</td><td>300 to 375 dollars</td><td>12 to 15 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Site prep and cleanup</td><td>150 to 200 dollars</td><td>6 to 8 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>2,350 to 2,775 dollars</td><td>100 percent</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These percentages shift somewhat depending on paver material choice, pattern complexity, and site conditions, but this gives a realistic sense of where the money in an installed quote actually goes.</p>
<h2><b>What Increases Installed Cost Above the Baseline</b></h2>
<h3><b>Pattern Complexity</b></h3>
<p>Herringbone and basketweave patterns require more cutting and precise alignment than running bond or stack bond, which adds labor time even when material costs stay the same. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> covers how these patterns differ in installation difficulty.</p>
<h3><b>Curved Layouts</b></h3>
<p>Curved walkways require significantly more individual paver cutting and fitting than straight ones, typically adding 15 to 30 percent above an equivalent straight walkway’s cost. Our<a href="/blog/how-to-make-a-curved-paver-walkway/"> guide to curved paver walkways</a> covers what drives that added cost in more detail.</p>
<h3><b>Slopes and Grading</b></h3>
<p>Sloped sites require additional excavation, deeper base work, and sometimes drainage improvements like a French drain, all of which add labor and material cost above a flat, straightforward site. Our<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway-on-a-slope/"> guide to building on a slope</a> covers why this terrain adds real complexity to a project.</p>
<h3><b>Paver Material Choice</b></h3>
<p>Standard concrete pavers sit at the lower end of material cost, while brick and natural stone generally cost more both in material price and in the labor required to work with them. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-brick-paver-walkway/"> building a brick paver walkway</a> covers some of the material-specific cost factors that come with that choice.</p>
<h3><b>Soil Conditions</b></h3>
<p>Heavy clay soil, common in parts of the Denver area, often requires deeper base depth to properly handle seasonal ground movement, which adds material and labor cost compared to a site with simpler, well-draining soil.</p>

